{
  "assessment": "The paper provides a quantitative model for the static rolling friction of liquid marbles, with measured departure angles as a function of volume and surface fraction, and fitted parameters. It supports a numeric control (volume, surface fraction) and readout (departure angle, friction force), with mechanisms and equations. Case authoring is ready.",
  "case_authoring_ready": true,
  "source": {
    "citation": "Yui Takai, Static Friction of Liquid Marbles, Physical Review Letters (2026), DOI:10.1103/njnj-yhc6",
    "journal_doi": "10.1103/njnj-yhc6",
    "arxiv_versioned_id": "2506.20116v2"
  },
  "paper_scope": "The paper studies the static rolling friction of liquid marbles (water droplets coated with lycopodium particles) on tilted substrates. It measures the departure angle as a function of marble volume and particle surface fraction, and models the friction as arising from shear yielding of the granular shell. It also measures the yielding force of two-dimensional granular rafts to probe the surface-fraction dependence of the intergrain force function f(phi).",
  "mechanisms": [
    {
      "mechanism_id": "rolling_friction_energy_balance",
      "statement": "The onset of rolling of a liquid marble is governed by an energy balance: the gravitational energy gain during rolling must exceed the shear dissipation in the granular shell. The shell is modeled as a purely plastic, quasi-static granular raft with yield stress q_y = mu p(phi), where mu is a macroscopic friction coefficient and p(phi) is the mean normal stress. The effective surface tension is gamma_eff = gamma(1 - f(phi)), with f(phi) a dimensionless function of surface fraction. This yields the departure angle alpha approximately (S a^2 / Omega) (delta_epsilon/delta_x) mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi)).",
      "equations": [
        "alpha ≈ (S a^2 / Omega) (delta_epsilon/delta_x) mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi))",
        "q_y = mu p(phi)",
        "gamma_eff = gamma(1 - f(phi))"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "alpha",
        "S",
        "a",
        "Omega",
        "delta_epsilon",
        "delta_x",
        "mu",
        "f(phi)",
        "q_y",
        "p(phi)",
        "gamma_eff",
        "gamma"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "mu_large_marbles",
          "value": 0.053,
          "uncertainty": "±0.003 (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "macroscopic friction coefficient fitted to large-marble departure angle data",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 115,
              "line_end": 115,
              "support": "Fitting Eq. (model_large) in the large-marble regime with f(phi_o)=0.26 and a(phi_o)=2.3 mm gives mu=0.053 ± 0.003."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "f_phi_o",
          "value": 0.26,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "value of f(phi) at initial surface fraction phi_o=0.83, used in fitting",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 115,
              "line_end": 115,
              "support": "Fitting Eq. (model_large) with f(phi_o)=0.26 and a(phi_o)=2.3 mm."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "a_phi_o",
          "value": 0.0023,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "m",
          "role": "capillary length at phi_o, used in fitting",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 115,
              "line_end": 115,
              "support": "Fitting Eq. (model_large) with a(phi_o)=2.3 mm."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 86,
          "line_end": 101,
          "support": "Departure angle derived from energy balance: gravitational energy gain exceeds shear dissipation in granular shell; shell modeled as plastic with yield stress q_y=mu p(phi); effective surface tension gamma_eff=gamma(1-f(phi)); final equation alpha ≈ (S a^2/Omega)(delta_epsilon/delta_x) mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "large_marble_model",
      "statement": "For large marbles flattened by gravity to height ~2a, approximating the shape as a cylinder of base diameter l and height 2a gives Omega ≈ pi l^2 a/2, sheared area S ≈ pi l^2/2 + 2 pi l a, and shear strain delta_epsilon ≈ delta_x/(2a). Combining with the general energy balance yields the departure angle alpha ≈ (1/2)(1 + sqrt(8 pi a^3/Omega)) mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi)).",
      "equations": [
        "Omega ≈ pi l^2 a/2",
        "S ≈ pi l^2/2 + 2 pi l a",
        "delta_epsilon ≈ delta_x/(2a)",
        "alpha ≈ (1/2)(1 + sqrt(8 pi a^3/Omega)) mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi))"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "Omega",
        "l",
        "a",
        "S",
        "delta_epsilon",
        "delta_x",
        "alpha",
        "mu",
        "f(phi)"
      ],
      "parameters": [],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 105,
          "line_end": 113,
          "support": "Large-marble regime: gravity flattens marbles to height ~2a; cylinder approximation gives Omega≈pi l^2 a/2, S≈pi l^2/2+2 pi l a, delta_epsilon≈delta_x/(2a); combining with general model gives alpha≈(1/2)(1+sqrt(8 pi a^3/Omega)) mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "small_marble_model",
      "statement": "For small quasi-spherical marbles of radius R, with Omega ≈ 4 pi R^3/3, balancing weight with Laplace pressure gives l ~ R^2/a. Shear is confined near the contact over distance l, so S ≈ 2 pi R l and delta_epsilon ≈ delta_x/l. This yields alpha ≈ (6 pi^2)^{1/3} (a^3/Omega)^{2/3} mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi)).",
      "equations": [
        "Omega ≈ 4 pi R^3/3",
        "l ~ R^2/a",
        "S ≈ 2 pi R l",
        "delta_epsilon ≈ delta_x/l",
        "alpha ≈ (6 pi^2)^{1/3} (a^3/Omega)^{2/3} mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi))"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "Omega",
        "R",
        "l",
        "a",
        "S",
        "delta_epsilon",
        "delta_x",
        "alpha",
        "mu",
        "f(phi)"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "small_marble_prefactor",
          "value": 0.69,
          "uncertainty": "not reported",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "empirical prefactor adjusting the small-marble model to data",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 122,
              "line_end": 122,
              "support": "Using mu obtained for large marbles, Eq. (model_small) predicts the small-Omega data with a prefactor of 0.69."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 117,
          "line_end": 121,
          "support": "Small-marble model: Omega≈4 pi R^3/3, l~R^2/a, S≈2 pi R l, delta_epsilon≈delta_x/l, yielding alpha≈(6 pi^2)^{1/3}(a^3/Omega)^{2/3} mu f(phi)/(1-f(phi))."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "logistic_f_phi",
      "statement": "The function f(phi), which quantifies the surface-fraction dependence of the intergrain normal force, is described by a logistic function: f(phi) = 1/(1 + exp[-beta(phi - phi_c)]), with phi_c = phi_o - (1/beta) ln(gamma/gamma_eff(phi_o) - 1). For liquid marbles, beta = 15.4 ± 1.7 and phi_c ≈ 0.90 ± 0.01. This logistic form is also observed for granular rafts, with material- and run-dependent parameters.",
      "equations": [
        "f(phi) = 1/(1 + exp[-beta(phi - phi_c)])",
        "phi_c = phi_o - (1/beta) ln(gamma/gamma_eff(phi_o) - 1)"
      ],
      "variable_names": [
        "f(phi)",
        "beta",
        "phi_c",
        "phi_o",
        "gamma",
        "gamma_eff"
      ],
      "parameters": [
        {
          "name": "beta_marbles",
          "value": 15.4,
          "uncertainty": "±1.7 (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "steepness parameter of logistic f(phi) for liquid marbles",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 155,
              "line_end": 155,
              "support": "Data collapse onto alpha/alpha_o = exp[beta(phi - phi_o)] with beta = 15.4 ± 1.7."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "name": "phi_c_marbles",
          "value": 0.9,
          "uncertainty": "±0.01 (source_unspecified)",
          "unit": "dimensionless",
          "role": "inflection point of logistic f(phi) for liquid marbles",
          "anchors": [
            {
              "line_start": 164,
              "line_end": 164,
              "support": "phi_c ≈ 0.90 ± 0.01."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "anchors": [
        {
          "line_start": 159,
          "line_end": 164,
          "support": "From experimental result and Eq. (model_large) in asymptotic limit, f(phi) = 1/(1+e^{-beta(phi-phi_c)}) with phi_c = phi_o - (1/beta) ln(gamma/gamma_eff(phi_o)-1), giving phi_c ≈ 0.90 ± 0.01."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "mechanism_id": "raft_yielding_force",
      "statement": "For two-dimensional granular rafts, the yielding force F_y measured by dragging a fiber is proportional to f(phi). Normalizing by its asymptotic value F_y^max gives f(phi) = F_y(phi)/F_y^max, which follows a logistic function with parameters (F_max, beta, phi_c) that vary with particle type and run.",
      "equations": [
        "F_y ~ f(phi)",
        "f(phi) = F_y(phi)/F_y^max",
        "F_y(phi) = F_max / [1 + exp[-beta(phi - phi_c)]]"
      ],
